Make the logical slice visible in `npu-smi info`, and keep that output clean.

WHAT: adds src/dsmi/dsmi_hook.c, a strong `dsmi_get_hbm_info` definition, and compiles
it into libvruntime.so only (not into enpu-monitor). The hook passes every call through
to the driver and then rewrites two fields for the sliced device: the HBM total becomes
the container's `memory-quota` (clamped to the card's real total) and the HBM usage
becomes the slice's own usage — the number `enpu-monitor` prints. `ENPU_DSMI_HOOK`
gates it; absent means off. On its non-CANN path the hook also drops
`g_log_config.min_log_level` to FATAL for the span of its vcann-rt helper calls, then puts it
back.

WHY: vcann-rt hooks the CANN runtime (rt*) layer, and `npu-smi` never enters it — it
links libdrvdsmi_host.so + libascend_hal.so and imports zero rt symbols, so a sliced
container's `npu-smi info` reports the whole physical card. Measured on a 910B2 (driver
25.5.1): interposing this one dsmi getter moves `HBM Capacity(MB)` from 65536 to 32768,
and libvruntime.so is already an entry of the sliced container's /etc/ld.so.preload, so
no extra library, mount or preload entry is needed.

WHY THE LOG THRESHOLD: `log_print()` returns early when `level > min_log_level`, and only
after that gate does it check `g_log_initialized` — printing "[eNPU] Log module not
initialized, cannot print log." to stderr in place of the message. The pre-init default is
INFO, and `log_init()` runs only from the `rtSetDevice*` hooks, which a non-CANN process
never reaches; so every INFO/WARN the helpers emit lands on that fprintf, seven lines deep
into a plain `npu-smi info`. A threshold of FATAL makes each `LOG_` macro fail its own
`min_log_level >= level` gate, so `log_print` is never even entered, and `LOG_FATAL` has no
call site anywhere in the tree. Nothing is lost either way: an uninitialized log module
cannot print its message at any level.

The threshold is FATAL and not one below it. `EnpuLogLevel` declares no negative
enumerator, so the compiler gives it an unsigned type and `ENPU_LOG_FATAL - 1` converts to
UINT_MAX — which passes every gate and would print MORE than changing nothing at all,
including the DEBUG lines the default INFO threshold suppresses. Measured with the real
header: 7 noise lines before, 10 with UINT_MAX, 0 with FATAL.

The threshold is RESTORED rather than left lowered, because neither of the two things that
might otherwise restore it does. `log_init()` begins with `if (g_log_initialized) return`,
so once the module is up it never revisits `parse_level_env()`. And `check_init_success()`
reports npu-manager's own flag, not the log module's, so it is false in a CANN process
whose log module is running but whose global init failed — the process that most needs its
diagnostics. Bounding the change to the helper calls keeps that process's configured level.
The suppression deliberately does not extend to `dsmi_get_hbm_info`'s own per-call helper:
flipping a shared global on every call would risk swallowing another thread's log line,
which is worse than the noise it would remove. The same trade-off applies in miniature to
the one-time window here, which is entered under `pthread_once` — a thread logging inside
it loses that line, and the store to `g_log_config.min_log_level` is unsynchronized, as
upstream's own writes to it are.

This hook's own fprintf diagnostics are direct rather than routed through log_print, so
they are unaffected and still reach the operator.

AGAINST: ubs-virt 9f8c779685edeb0939a681922bb56f09128f3445 (the pin in
pack/gpustack-operator/Dockerfile), subdir ubs-virt-enpu/vcann-rt. Applied by
external/ascend/build-libvnpu.sh with `patch -p1` in its work copy of that subdir.

REFRESHED from 476bb968d477a4eab7719197af212c6c3470ff0b for that pin, whose 310P dcmi
adapter replaced `soc_version`'s `SOC_VERSION_NOT_ASCEND_950` with `SOC_VERSION_ASCEND_910`
plus `SOC_VERSION_ASCEND_310`. This hook takes the 910 name and does not discriminate,
because `register_callback()` gives 910 and 310 the same
`get_device_resource_info_callback` and that is the only callback used here. Compiling the
old patch against the new pin produced that one error and no other.

LINE ENDINGS: upstream's CMakeLists.txt is CRLF, so this file's CMakeLists hunk carries
CRLF context and must stay byte-exact - .gitattributes pins `-text` on it, because the
repo's `eol=lf` normalisation would otherwise strip those CRs and the hunk would stop
matching. Keep both facts together if either changes.

TO REFRESH after a pin bump: clone the new pin, apply this patch, resolve the reject,
then regenerate with `git add -N src/dsmi/dsmi_hook.c && git diff --relative` from the
vcann-rt subdir and keep this header on top. Upstream would be the better home for it
(see the spec's Open Question 1), which would let this file be dropped.

